{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act causes for criminal offenses, theft and human trafficking","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or found civilly liable for, assault, threats, theft, drug activity or human trafficking on the premises, or who knowingly houses such a person. A three-day notice to quit is required; most suits are barred after two years.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit; 2-year limit on suits","coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies covered by the Eviction Law; the causes apply to a tenant or to a person the tenant knowingly permits to reside at the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"The civil-court cause does not apply to a tenant allowing a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside in the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found by the court to be delinquent due to an offense involving assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, a member of the landl ord’s family or an employee of the landlord.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction causes and notice periods for landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"To evict from an owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling, a court order and proof of holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, willful damage, constant rule violations or a re-entry lease violation are required. Notice: three months (at-will/yearly), one month (month-to-month).","key_value":"3-month notice (at-will/yearly); 1-month (month-to-month); 3-day for disorderly, destructive or rule-violating conduct","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; the landlord must prove one of the listed causes in court.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction causes and notices","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations after owner-occupancy or removal-from-use evictions","requirement":"A landlord who evicted a tenant for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months or owe three times damages. A landlord who evicted to board up, demolish or retire a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6-month owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use ban; 3x damages; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation","coverage_conditions":"Units vacated after a notice that the landlord will personally occupy the unit or permanently board up, demolish or retire the property from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"evictions for owner occupancy or removal from residential use"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000.00 per violation; the property may not be registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord seeks to permanently board up or demolish the building or to permanently retire it from residential use, the landlord must not use this property for residential use for a period of five (5) years.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal eviction prohibited when tenant exercises civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter a lease or its terms, or refuse to renew a lease in reprisal because a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, makes a good-faith complaint to a government authority, or organizes or joins a tenant organization.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"excludes owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction protections and notice requirements","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. A buyer who wants to occupy a foreclosed unit as a primary residence must give the tenant a 90-day notice to quit and honor a lease running beyond 90 days; new owners must notify tenants within 10 business days.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; tenant notices within 10 business days of the sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants of foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act protection from conversion eviction","requirement":"Tenants who are at least 62, permanently disabled or certain disabled veterans may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is within limits; converters must notify eligible tenants and applications generally must be filed within 60 days.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income limit three times county average per-person income or $50,000.00, whichever is greater; 60-day filing","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62, permanently disabled, or disabled veterans (60% or more service-connected) who lived in a building being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"tenants in buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy in qualified counties","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Act of 1992 extends protected tenancy, in buildings converted or being converted, to qualified tenants in qualified counties who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act; currently only Hudson County is a qualified county.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Tenants in qualified counties, in buildings converted or being converted, who were not eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"qualified counties; currently only Hudson County"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on rent increases above rent control or unreasonable rent increases","requirement":"A landlord may not increase rent in excess of a rent control or rent leveling ordinance; where the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase is prohibited.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental housing subject to a rent control or rent leveling ordinance, and rental property not subject to rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Department of Community Affairs, Division of Housing and Community Resources","quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
